<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
<phyphox version="1.20" locale="en">
  <title>Motion Lab</title>
  <category>HBCSE-LLNL Labs</category>
  <icon>avx</icon>
  <color>168AAD</color>
  <description>Turns the phone into a velocity and displacement meter. Record the phone at rest and then moving; the module removes the sensor offset and integrates acceleration to velocity and displacement (or angular velocity to angle) for any part of the recording. Requires phyphox 1.2.1 or newer.

HOW TO USE
Keep the phone's orientation fixed. Press Play, hold the phone still for a few seconds, make one short, controlled movement along one axis, hold still again, then pause. Choose the sensor (acceleration without g, acceleration with g, or gyroscope) and the axis. Drag the green slider over the resting part and press Calculate/Remove correction. Drag the blue slider over the movement and enter the initial velocity (0 if the selected part starts at rest). Use Pick data on the graphs to read values. Clear the recording with the trash bin before a new run, and do not integrate across pauses.

THE CORRECTION BUTTON
One button whose label changes. Calculate/Remove correction: nothing is applied yet (at the start, after the trash bin, or after changing the sensor or axis); pressing it calculates the correction. Remove correction: a correction is applied; pressing it sets the correction to zero, so the drift of the uncorrected signal becomes visible. Calculate correction: shown after removing, or after moving the green slider; pressing it calculates the correction from the green region again. The value below the button shows the correction that is currently applied.

WHAT IS SHOWN
Upper graph: the raw readings, with the correction region in green and the integration region in blue. Corrected graph: raw minus correction over the blue region; the gray line marks zero. Below it, velocity (or angle for the gyroscope) and displacement (orange line) with the distance along the axis (magenta dots). Velocity starts at the initial velocity you enter; displacement and angle start at zero. These graphs stay empty until a correction has been calculated or removed. The Raw tab shows X, Y, Z and the magnitude of the selected sensor for the whole recording, and the recorded sample rate.

METHOD
The correction is the mean of the readings in the green region, subtracted once from the original readings. Integration uses the trapezoidal rule with the real timestamps: v[i] = v[i-1] + (a[i-1] + a[i])·Δt/2 and x[i] = x[i-1] + (v[i-1] + v[i])·Δt/2. The distance along the axis integrates the absolute velocity, handling changes of direction correctly; it is not the 3D path length. All sensors run at the fastest rate the phone allows, with no averaging or smoothing. The analysis uses the first 100 s of a recording; the whole recording is kept and exported.

LIMITS
Any leftover offset grows into drift, linearly in velocity and quadratically in displacement, so keep movements short. Subtracting the resting value removes gravity only while the orientation stays fixed. The axes are fixed to the phone. For the gyroscope, the angle is meaningful only for rotation about one fixed axis, and there is no displacement graph.

Created by Praveen Pathak and David Rakestraw with AI assistance.
Contact: Praveen Pathak, praveen@hbcse.tifr.res.in
All modules and updates: https://github.com/praveen2600/phyphox-modules</description><link label="Email the author">mailto:praveen@hbcse.tifr.res.in</link><link label="All modules on GitHub">https://github.com/praveen2600/phyphox-modules</link>
